Sure! Here's the response based on the provided information, translated into English:

Hello mgravell,

After an in-depth analysis, I discovered that the mistake was indeed on my end. I've been using a custom library to fluently generate SQL queries. Upon reviewing the code for this library, I pinpointed the error within the Values method of the SqlQueryBuilder.

Library: SqlQueryBuilder

```
public class SqlQueryBuilder
{
    // ... (other methods)

    public SqlQueryBuilder Values(params string[] values)
    {
        _query.Append(" VALUES (");
        _query.Append(string.Join(", ", values.Select(v => $"'{v}'")));
        _query.Append(")");
        return this;
    }
    // ... (other methods)
}

```

Identified Issue:
The Values method was appending single quotes (' ') around each value passed to it, regardless of whether it was a literal value or a parameter placeholder (like @UserId). This led to the incorrect formation of the SQL query, as mentioned.

For example, the erroneous string generated was:

`"INSERT INTO [User] (UserId, Username, Password, Email, Role, CreatedAt, LastUpdated) VALUES ('@UserId', '@Username', '@Password', '@Email', '@Role', '@CreatedAt', '@LastUpdated')"`

Solution:
I modified the Values method to check if a value is a parameter placeholder (starting with @). If it's a placeholder, it doesn't wrap it in single quotes.

```
public SqlQueryBuilder Values(params string[] values)
{
    _query.Append(" VALUES (");
    _query.Append(string.Join(", ", values.Select(v => v.StartsWith("@") ? v : $"'{v}'")));
    _query.Append(")");
    return this;
}

```

With this fix, the library now generates the SQL query correctly, and parameter values are handled appropriately.

I apologize for the oversight and thank you for bringing this issue to my attention. The correction has been implemented and tested. If you have any further feedback or encounter any more issues, please let me know!

Kind regards,

Fernando Lotti

