Depending on the type of DB provider you're using, you could probably write something like the following in your SQL command (this is a SQL server example):

```
string query = "
Update table1 set tbcol=@tbcol where...;
Set @table1cnt = @@rowcount;

 Update table2 set tbcol=@tbcol where...;
Set @table2cnt = @@rowcount";
```

Then, you could do one of two things:

1. Define table1cnt and table2cnt as output parameters and get their values.
2. Declare @table1cnt and @table2cnt in the beginning of the SQL command, and at the end use SELECT statement to return their values as if a query. I.e.: `SELECT @table1cnt as t1cnt, @table2cnt as t2cnt`


The usage of @@rowcount global variable is a SQL Server syntax, but I'm certain something like this is possible in all popular DB providers as well.

