MySQL Server doesn't store any time zone information. As per [time zone support](https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.0/en/time-zone-support.html):

> The current session time zone setting affects display and storage of time values that are zone-sensitive. This includes the values displayed by functions such as NOW() or CURTIME(), and values stored in and retrieved from TIMESTAMP columns. Values for TIMESTAMP columns are converted from the current time zone to UTC for storage, and from UTC to the current time zone for retrieval.

When a DateTime column value is sent from the MySQL Server to the client, it's sent without any time zone information. Connector/NET (i.e., MySql.Data) tries to synthesize the time zone based on some heuristics about the difference between the server's and client's time zone. You can see the history of how this has changed in these bugs: [63812](https://bugs.mysql.com/bug.php?id=63812), [66964](https://bugs.mysql.com/bug.php?id=66964).

I think you're running into a (as yet unreported?) bug in Connector/NET where the value of `MySqlDataReader.GetDateTime()` differs from `MySqlDataReader.GetValue()`. This can be demonstrated with the following code:

```csharp
using (var connection = new MySqlConnection("..."))
{
	connection.Open();
	
	using (var cmd = new MySqlCommand("select * from hello_world.timeZomeTest;", connection))
	using (var reader = cmd.ExecuteReader())
	{
		reader.Read();
		Console.WriteLine(reader.GetDateTime(2).Kind); // Local
		
		Console.WriteLine(((DateTime) reader.GetValue(2)).Kind); // Utc
	}

	var tz = connection.Query<TimeZomeTest>("select * from hello_world.timeZomeTest").Single();
	Console.WriteLine(tz.ModifiedDate.Kind); // Utc
}
```

Dapper just reports the `DateTimeKind` that is provided to it by Connector/NET; it relies on the database connection library to report correct values.

FWIW, [MySqlConnector](https://github.com/mysql-net/MySqlConnector) doesn't have any time zone heuristics and just reports `Unspecified` for all the examples above. It does provide a `DateTimeKind` connection string option if you'd like to force Local or Utc on the returned values. (This isn't always appropriate; for example, `select utc_timestamp(), current_timestamp();` will return two timestamps with different (implied) time zones.)

