@rexlam0630 you need to understand a bit more about how async/await really works. When you call `ExecuteAsync()`, the method starts executing immediately (and synchronously). When it hits the first yielding await (i.e. I/O) operation, the method will stop executing and set up a callback to run when the operation completes.

When you write the following code:

```c#
var tasks = new List<Task> {
    conn.ExecuteAsync(sql1, param1, transaction),
    conn.ExecuteAsync(sql2, param2, transaction),
    conn.ExecuteAsync(sql3, param3, transaction),
    conn.ExecuteAsync(sql4, param3, transaction)
};
```

The first `ExecuteAsync()` starts executing, and then yields when it first does any I/O. Since you did not await it, our thread continues to the second `ExecuteAsync()`, which fails immediately because the first one is still in progress.

Finally, regarding inserting multiple items... I don't know Dapper very well, but at the SQL level you can simply do `INSERT INTO table (x, y) VALUES (1,2), (3,4)` and you've inserted two rows in a single statement (and single network roundtrip). You can also pack multiple statements into a single command by setting CommandText to `INSERT INTO table (x, y) VALUES (1,2); INSERT INTO table (x, y) VALUES (3,4)`, which will again work in a single network roundtrip. If you're inserting many rows, the second option can be better since it can benefit from preparation (but read [the Npgsql docs](http://www.npgsql.org/doc/prepare.html) to understand that better). Finally, if you're really inserting lots of rows and are interested in maximum perf, look at [Npgsql's bulk copy support](http://www.npgsql.org/doc/copy.html), which is the fastest possible method (but works totally outside Dapper).

All of the above has nothing to do with *transactions*, but rather about inserting multiple rows in a single network roundtrip (which may or may not correspond to a transaction).

